<p id="p2">I spent yesterday in New York City interviewing developers about their iPhone Duo apps and the experience of building for such a new and different device. The depth and breadth of developers Apple assembled was excellent. I spoke to indie developers like Vidit Bhargava about <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/deck-man-cassette-player/id6817070335" rel="noopener noreferrer">Deck-Man</a> and the team behind <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/notewise-ai-notes-pdf-docs/id6480045936" rel="noopener noreferrer">Notewise</a>. I also interviewed developers from big companies about their impressive Duo updates, including The New York Times for its <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/nyt-cooking-quick-tasty-meals/id911422904" rel="noopener noreferrer">NYT Cooking app</a>, <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/netflix/id363590051" rel="noopener noreferrer">Netflix</a>, and Meta’s <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/edits-video-editor/id6738967378" rel="noopener noreferrer">Edits</a> video editing app. Plus, I met Mark Otero from Azra Games, whose team poured their hearts into <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/ungodly/id6740617332" rel="noopener noreferrer">UnGodly</a>, one of the best-looking games I’ve seen on iOS in a while.</p>
<p id="p3">A few things struck me about my conversations with developers. First, every one of them was excited about the design challenges posed by the iPhone Duo and the many orientations and ways it can be used. Second, every one of them came away impressed by how smooth Apple has made the development process. That’s not to say the design challenges weren’t real, but getting from a design idea to an app that worked on the Duo went better than anyone expected. Third, after months of building and testing, everyone was looking forward to seeing which design choices land with users.</p>
